import { writeFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { roleTemplates, permissionLabels } from "../src/lib/permissions.ts";
const matrix = Object.entries(roleTemplates)
  .map(
    ([name, t]) =>
      `| ${name} | ${t.scope.replaceAll("_", " ")} | ${t.permissions.length ? t.permissions.map((p) => permissionLabels[p] || p).join("; ") : "Self-service only"} | ${t.note} |`,
  )
  .join("\n");
writeFileSync(
  "docs/PERMISSIONS.md",
  `# Permission matrix\n\nJob classification, position, department, reporting relationship, functional role, and access scope are separate records. Changing a job title or classification never grants permissions. The following templates are applied only through an explicit authorized assignment. Runtime access checks use the resulting permission-grant rows and the current organization records.\n\n## Baseline for every active employee\n\n- Own work profile and permitted private personal-information updates (personal email, address, emergency contact). Work titles, department, reporting manager, status, and roles remain administrator-controlled.\n- Own clock-in/out, attendance history, schedules, correction requests, leave balances, leave requests, and overtime requests.\n- Own published payslips; own drafts and other employees’ pay are not included.\n- Own personnel documents, tasks, onboarding/cleaning checklists, training, assigned reviews, feedback, notifications, and acknowledgements.\n- Company announcements and company policies applicable to their department.\n- Bank, government, medical, disciplinary, and identity categories still require their own explicit permission, including when the record belongs to the employee.\n\n## Initial templates\n\nAll rows include baseline self-service. Suggested scopes must be explicitly chosen during assignment. A title never supplies approval authority.\n\n| Profile / functional role | Suggested scope | Additional permissions | Limits and purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n${matrix}\n\n## Scope rules\n\n| Scope | Record match |\n| --- | --- |\n| Own | Target employee is the employee linked to the authenticated account. |\n| Direct reports | Target employee’s stored manager ID equals the authenticated employee ID. This does not include indirect reports. |\n| Assigned team | Target employee’s primary team matches the specific team ID on the grant. |\n| Assigned department | Target employee’s department matches the specific department ID on the grant. Department ancestry does not imply inherited access. |\n| Company | All eligible records within this single-company deployment. |\n\nFor multiple assigned teams or departments, add one scoped assignment per team or department. Department-scoped planning uses the plan’s target department. Organization governance and permission-grant administration require company-scope permission. Account operations are separately authorized from HR record access.\n\n## Combinations\n\n- **Specialist + HR Officer, People & Culture department:** self-service plus the HR Officer grants for employees in that department. No automatic payroll, medical, disciplinary, bank, government-ID, or identity-file access.\n- **Managerial, Engineering team + Payroll Administrator, Finance department:** team directory/attendance/approval responsibilities for Engineering, plus payroll responsibilities only for employees in the explicitly assigned Finance department. Final approvals still need an approval-route assignment.\n- **Owner + Technical Administrator, company:** governance and account operations. Confidential HR and payroll still require explicit additional grants. This is the first-admin bootstrap combination.\n\n## Approval authority\n\nA decision requires an active account/session, required MFA, a matching current permission and scope, and the explicit approver or valid delegate assignment. Requesters cannot approve their own requests. The initial leave.approve capability covers assigned leave and overtime decisions; attendance corrections use attendance.manage; headcount plans use planning.manage. Supervisor recommendations do not finalize a request. Reviewer assignments authorize review feedback only for the assigned employee.\n\nTemporary leave/correction delegates must hold the relevant scoped permission, and their stored delegation period must still be valid. Request records retain the approver and delegate selected at submission. A route change does not silently rewrite existing requests.\n\n## Field and document boundaries\n\n| Data | Read boundary | Write boundary |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Work directory | Own record, employees.read within scope, or applicable assigned-review context | employees.manage for both current and destination scope |\n| Personal contact/address/emergency information | Own record or private.read within scope; audited RPC | Own permitted fields or private.write within scope; audited RPC |\n| Payslips/compensation | Own published statement or explicit payroll authorization; audited RPC, no raw table SELECT | payroll.manage; create/import drafts, explicit publication |\n| Company documents | Active employees in the applicable company/department audience | documents.manage at the company/department audience |\n| Personnel documents | Own file or documents.read within scope | documents.manage within scope |\n| Bank details | sensitive.bank.read within scope | sensitive.bank.write within scope |\n| Government identifiers | sensitive.government.read within scope | sensitive.government.write within scope |\n| Medical records | sensitive.medical.read within scope | sensitive.medical.write within scope |\n| Disciplinary records | sensitive.disciplinary.read within scope | sensitive.disciplinary.write within scope |\n| Identity files | sensitive.identity.read within scope | sensitive.identity.write within scope |\n| Review feedback | Employee, assigned reviewer, or scoped performance.read; audited RPC | Employee reflection or assigned reviewer feedback; HR assigns reviewers |\n| Accounts | Account/access administration only; employee work identity for account operations | accounts.manage; no self-suspension or self-deactivation |\n| Permission grants | Own grants or access.manage | access.manage; no login/registration role fields |\n| Audit events | Company-scope audit.read | Internal audited functions only |\n\nAll elevated permission grants require MFA. The role label itself is not checked for authorization. Removing a grant changes access on the next request. Deactivation, logout, expiration, or revocation invalidates the application session even when the client still holds a previously issued JWT. Raw sensitive tables, Auth tickets, rate-limit keys, and storage objects are inaccessible through ordinary authenticated/anonymous API calls.\n`,
);
console.log("Generated the permission matrix from the actual application templates.");
